# This guide's own readings where it weighs the evidence itself instead of only reporting Catane.
# Rule (Mordy, 2026-10-06): originality is wanted as long as it is grounded. Every entry names its grounds:
# Rashi's own words elsewhere, the letters of the printed text, or spelling habits visible in Catane's own entries.
# kind: "differs" = the guide reads the word differently from Catane; "sides" = the guide weighs a dispute and sides with Catane for a stated reason;
# "moved" = the guide corrects where the entry is filed.
OWN={
('Genesis','3002'):("differs","Catane gives the dictionary sense, to cover. This guide reads it as the bird's word: to sit over a nest. The grounds are Rashi's own. Here he says the Throne hovered like a dove hovering over the nest. At Devarim 32:11, on the same Hebrew verb, he says what that hovering is: the bird does not rest its weight on the young but touches and does not touch (אינו מכביד עצמו עליהם אלא מחופף, נוגע ואינו נוגע). A plain covering loses exactly that. Old French had cover for a bird brooding beside covrir for covering, and Rashi's picture calls for the first. If so, English incubate is a true relative. Manuscript Leipzig 1 cannot help here: the opening of the chapter is missing from it."),
('Genesis','3009'):("sides","The printed letters can be read as they stand: machedure, a bruise. An earlier draft of this guide defended that. Two things decide against it. First, Rashi himself: at Shemot 21:25 he separates a petza, a blow that draws blood, from a chaburah, where the blood gathers and does not come out, and gives each its own French word; here too he defines petza as the stroke of a sword or an arrow. Second, the manuscript: Leipzig 1 reads נַברְדוּרָא here, vocalized, and calls it our language (בלשוננו). Catane's navredure is right."),
('Genesis','3041'):("sides","This guide first preferred destorner, the reading of the printed text, because Rashi's Hebrew explanation speaks of turning from one road to another. Manuscript Leipzig 1 reads דֵישְטוֹלִיד, vocalized, which is Catane's destolir family and not destorner. The manuscript outweighs the argument from sense, so the guide withdraws its reading and follows Catane. Destorner looks like a later copyist's easier word."),
('Exodus','3103'):("sides","Rashi's definition here, a blow that draws blood, is the key to the disputed word at Bereshit 4:23. See that card."),
('Exodus','3104'):("sides","This guide first read the printed טק\"א as tache, a stain, taking ק for the French ch. Manuscript Leipzig 1 reads טייא, with no ק at all, which is Catane's spelling. The printed ק is therefore a copyist's letter and cannot carry the argument. The guide withdraws its reading and follows Catane."),
('Numbers','3231'):("sides","This guide first read the printed פורי\"ר as forer, the ordinary French verb to bore. Manuscript Leipzig 1 reads פורְיְיֵר, vocalized, with the doubled yod of Catane's forjier. So the longer form is the old one and the printed spelling is a shortening. The meaning is not in doubt: Catane translates it to bore, and Rashi ties it to digging out. The guide follows Catane on the form."),
('Leviticus','3192'):("moved","The digital text of Catane files this entry at verse 5. His own note shows it belongs to verse 16, on rachil: he explains that Rashi links the talebearer to the foot as French links espier to pie. The printed Rashi has its French word for this at verse 16, and so does manuscript Leipzig 1 (אֶשפיאְמֵנ\"ט). The card has been moved there. An earlier version of this guide guessed that Catane's Hebrew meaning, spying, was a slip; that guess was wrong and his note settles it. The French word that the printed Rashi does give at verse 5, אפיי\"צימנטו, has no entry of its own in Catane's list."),
}
